1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:11,863
In the kingdom of Pañchāla, King Drupada kindled a great sacrificial fire, and out of the flames rose a daughter —

2
00:00:11,863 --> 00:00:16,819
dark, radiant, born of no womb at all. Her name was Draupadī.

3
00:00:16,819 --> 00:00:21,450
The gods whispered that she would bend the fate of kings.

4
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:30,703
When she came of age, kings from every land filled Drupada's hall.

5
00:00:30,703 --> 00:00:34,762
High above hung a golden fish, spinning on a wheel.

6
00:00:34,762 --> 00:00:43,198
Whoever could string the great bow, and pierce the fish's eye by its reflection alone, would win her hand.

7
00:00:47,198 --> 00:00:51,770
One by one, the proud kings strained against the bow —

8
00:00:51,770 --> 00:00:55,156
and one by one they fell back, defeated.

9
00:00:55,156 --> 00:01:00,998
Even Karṇa the golden rose to try; but Draupadī turned her face away.

10
00:01:04,998 --> 00:01:10,497
Then, from among the brahmins, a quiet young man in white rose to his feet —

11
00:01:10,497 --> 00:01:16,937
one of the five Pāṇḍava brothers the world believed dead in the burning of the lac house.

12
00:01:16,937 --> 00:01:18,384
His name was Arjuna.

13
00:01:22,784 --> 00:01:28,489
He circled the bow, and bowed his head. In a single breath he strung it.

14
00:01:28,489 --> 00:01:34,591
Then, gazing only into the water — at the trembling reflection of the wheel —

15
00:01:34,591 --> 00:01:37,364
he loosed five arrows into the sky.

16
00:01:43,164 --> 00:01:44,929
The golden fish fell.

17
00:01:44,929 --> 00:01:52,239
And Draupadī came to the stranger, and laid her garland of white flowers upon his neck.

18
00:01:52,239 --> 00:01:58,037
In the crowd, Kṛṣṇa smiled — for he alone had recognized the brahmin.

19
00:02:02,238 --> 00:02:08,526
That evening, at their mother's door, the brothers called out: "Mother —

20
00:02:08,526 --> 00:02:10,709
see what we have brought!

21
00:02:10,709 --> 00:02:16,560
" And Kuntī, without turning, answered: "Share it among yourselves.

22
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:21,101
" A mother's word, once spoken, could not be unsaid.

23
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:38,553
But the sage Vyāsa unfolded a deeper design: in a life before this one, Draupadī had prayed to Lord Śiva five times for a worthy husband, and Śiva had granted every prayer.

24
00:02:38,553 --> 00:02:46,445
So Draupadī wed all five Pāṇḍava brothers, and their fates were bound together until the end of the age.
